# Intro
A star lights the gas at rest inside a sphere around it, then a molecular cloud that
starts at a spherical face centred on the star (`0.25 L` from it) and moves radially away
from it. Light that crosses gas moving by more than three line widths
(`3 b`, `b = sqrt(2 k T / m_H2)`, 0.64 km/s at 50 K) relative to the column it carries sees
fresh absorption: the transport resets the carried column. The check reads particles
inside the cloud and compares their line transmission with the bar of the reset (the
column of the cloud alone) or of no reset (the column of all the gas from the star). Read
the README of `ISRFH2StarLit` for the build, the table and the parameters that the run holds
fixed.

# Run
```
./run.sh                       # cloud at 10 widths: expect the reset
widths=1 run_name=control ./run.sh   # cloud at 1 width: expect no reset
```
The run lasts 16 steps (`n_steps`, passed to swift as `-n`), not a fixed end time: the
light makes one hop per step, so the number of steps sets how far it reaches. The light then
crosses the cloud face by several hops and the check reads the last snapshot. Keep `n_steps` at
16 or below, and `dt_max` below `time_end / 16`, or the run reaches `time_end` first.
`makeIC.py --widths` sets the radial speed of the cloud in units of `b`. The check picks
the expected case from the speed written in `fixture.json`: more than 3 widths gives the
reset.

# Checking
For each particle in the cloud away from the box edge, the check compares the own
transmission of the LW light with the shielding function at the column expected for the
reset or for no reset, within a bar set by the hop of the transport. The speed of 1 width
is the control: a run that shows a reset there fails. `run.sh` calls
`checkH2Attenuation.py velocity`, which exits with a non-zero status when it fails.
